# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. Every file here is eventually read by a Linux container, and CRLF
# breaks several of them in ways whose error messages point somewhere else:
#
#   * A shell script with \r\n fails as
#     "exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint: no such file or directory" — the kernel
#     reads the shebang as "/bin/sh\r" and reports the interpreter missing, so
#     it looks like the file was never copied.
#   * A .env with \r\n leaves the carriage return *inside* the value, so
#     DB_PASSWORD=secret becomes "secret\r" and MySQL rejects the login with no
#     hint as to why.
#   * A Dockerfile with \r\n breaks backslash line-continuations.
#
# Normalising the whole tree to LF costs Windows editors nothing — Notepad has
# handled LF since 2018, and every other editor already did.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must never be touched, whatever the heuristic decides.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# Belt and braces for the ones that actually break. `text eol=lf` is explicit
# rather than relying on `text=auto` guessing these are text.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
.env* text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
